          <ol>
            <li><a href="#intro">Introduction</a></li>
            <li><a href="#argo">The Ship Argo Navis</a></li>
            <li><a href="#eridanus">The River Eridanus</a></li>
            <li><a href="#northern">Northern Members</a></li>
            <li><a href="#others">Columba & Pyxis</a></li>
            <li><a href="#deepsky">Deep-Sky Objects</a></li>
            <li><a href="#observation">Observing the Family</a></li>
            <li><a href="#facts">Interesting Facts</a></li>
            <li><a href="#resources">Resources</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>
      </aside>

      <!-- Main Content -->
      <div class="main-content">

        <!-- Answer Capsule -->
        <aside class="answer-capsule">
          <div class="capsule-icon">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5">
              <path d="M2 15c2 0 2 2 4 2s2-2 4-2 2 2 4 2 2-2 4-2 2 2 4 2"/>
              <path d="M2 19c2 0 2 2 4 2s2-2 4-2 2 2 4 2 2-2 4-2 2 2 4 2"/>
              <circle cx="9" cy="7" r="1" fill="currentColor"/>
              <circle cx="15" cy="6" r="1" fill="currentColor"/>
              <circle cx="12" cy="10" r="1" fill="currentColor"/>
              <path d="M9 7l3 3 3-4"/>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p>The Heavenly Waters gather nine constellations linked by water and the sea: the river Eridanus, the celestial dolphin Delphinus, the southern fish Piscis Austrinus, and the pieces of the great ship Argo Navis — Carina, Puppis, and Vela. Mostly southern, the family holds Canopus, the sky's second-brightest star, and the vast Carina Nebula.</p>
        </aside>

        <section id="intro" class="content-section">
          <h2>Introduction to the Heavenly Waters</h2>
          <p>Ancient sky-watchers imagined a whole region of the heavens given over to water — a cosmic ocean populated by rivers, fish, dolphins, and ships. The Heavenly Waters family collects these aquatic and nautical constellations into a single grouping, united not by one legend but by a shared watery theme that runs through many cultures' star lore.</p>
          <p>The bulk of the family lies in the southern sky, in the region the Greeks associated with the sea. Here sails the greatest ship of myth, and here a great river winds down toward the horizon. For northern observers, only a handful of these constellations climb into view — but those that do, like the charming little Dolphin and the lonely bright star Fomalhaut, are longtime favourites.</p>
          <p>This is also a family of astronomical superlatives. It contains the second-brightest star in the whole sky, one of the longest constellations, one of the largest and most active star-forming nebulae known, and the fascinating fragmented remains of a constellation so vast that astronomers eventually had to break it apart.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="argo" class="content-section">
          <h2>Argo Navis: The Ship Broken into Three</h2>
          <p>The centrepiece of the Heavenly Waters is a constellation that no longer officially exists. Argo Navis represented the ship on which Jason and the Argonauts sailed in quest of the Golden Fleece. It was one of Ptolemy's original 48 constellations — and by far the largest, sprawling across a huge stretch of the southern Milky Way.</p>
          <p>Its very size made it awkward. In the 1750s the French astronomer Nicolas Louis de Lacaille divided the unwieldy ship into more manageable pieces, and the modern constellations preserve that split:</p>
          <div class="info-box glass">
            <h3>The Pieces of Argo Navis</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Carina (the Keel):</strong> Holds Canopus, the sky's second-brightest star, and the immense Carina Nebula</li>
              <li><strong>Puppis (the Stern):</strong> A rich Milky Way constellation full of open star clusters</li>
              <li><strong>Vela (the Sails):</strong> Contains the Vela supernova remnant and the beautiful Eight-Burst planetary nebula</li>
              <li><strong>Pyxis (the Compass):</strong> A small later addition, representing the ship's mariner's compass</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p>Because the pieces were carved from a single constellation, their star designations were kept continuous across the fragments — a quirk that means each of Carina, Puppis, and Vela is missing some Greek-letter star names that belonged to the original whole. Argo Navis remains the only one of the classical constellations to have been retired.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="eridanus" class="content-section">
          <h2>Eridanus: The Celestial River</h2>
          <p>Eridanus is the great river of the sky, the sixth-largest constellation, winding in a long series of gentle curves from the foot of Orion deep into the far southern sky. In myth it is variously identified with the Nile, the Po, or the river into which Phaethon fell after losing control of the Sun's chariot.</p>
          <p>The river begins near brilliant Rigel in Orion and meanders southward for an enormous distance, finally reaching Achernar — "the end of the river" — a hot blue star and the ninth-brightest in the sky. Achernar spins so fast that it bulges dramatically at its equator, making it one of the least spherical stars known.</p>
          <p>Along its length Eridanus also passes Epsilon Eridani, one of the nearest sun-like stars at just 10.5 light-years, which is known to host at least one planet and a dusty debris disk — a system that has long fascinated those searching for worlds beyond our own.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="northern" class="content-section">
          <h2>The Northern Members: Delphinus, Equuleus and Piscis Austrinus</h2>
          <p>A few of the Heavenly Waters reach far enough north to be enjoyed from the northern hemisphere.</p>
          <h3>Delphinus — The Dolphin</h3>
          <p>Delphinus is a small but delightful constellation near the Summer Triangle, its compact diamond of stars genuinely suggesting a leaping dolphin. Two of its stars carry the whimsical names Sualocin and Rotanev — "Nicolaus Venator" spelled backwards, a playful signature left by a 19th-century astronomer's assistant. In myth the dolphin was a messenger of the sea god Poseidon.</p>
          <h3>Equuleus — The Little Horse</h3>
          <p>Equuleus is the second-smallest constellation of all, a faint little group beside Delphinus representing the head of a small horse or foal. Modest and easily overlooked, it is nonetheless one of the ancient 48.</p>
          <h3>Piscis Austrinus — The Southern Fish</h3>
          <p>Piscis Austrinus, the Southern Fish, is best known for Fomalhaut, a bright, isolated star that shines alone in an otherwise empty stretch of the northern autumn sky, earning it the nickname "the Lonely Star." Fomalhaut is encircled by a striking ring of dust and debris, within which one of the first directly imaged exoplanet candidates was found.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="others" class="content-section">
          <h2>Columba and the Southern Fragments</h2>
          <p>Rounding out the family are the smaller and fainter southern members:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Columba (the Dove):</strong> A modest southern constellation below Orion and Lepus, representing the dove that Noah — or, in the Greek tradition, the Argonauts — sent out to find land. It offers a gentle counterpoint to the sea imagery around it.</li>
            <li><strong>Pyxis (the Compass):</strong> A tiny constellation of the mariner's compass, added by Lacaille to accompany the pieces of the ship Argo. It sits in a rich part of the southern Milky Way.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Together with the ship fragments and the river, these constellations complete a coherent maritime scene painted across the southern heavens — a reminder of how deeply the sea shaped the imaginations of the cultures that first charted the stars.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="deepsky" class="content-section">
          <h2>Deep-Sky Treasures</h2>
          <p>Because so much of the family lies along the southern Milky Way, it is extraordinarily rich in clusters and nebulae — some of the finest objects in the entire sky.</p>
          <div class="info-box glass">
            <h3>Standout Objects</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Carina Nebula (NGC 3372):</strong> A colossal star-forming region around the unstable star Eta Carinae</li>
              <li><strong>Vela Supernova Remnant:</strong> The tattered shell of a star that exploded ~11,000 years ago, with a pulsar at its heart</li>
              <li><strong>Eight-Burst Nebula (NGC 3132):</strong> A bright planetary nebula in Vela, imaged in detail by James Webb</li>
              <li><strong>M46, M47 &amp; M93:</strong> Rich open star clusters in Puppis</li>
              <li><strong>Achernar:</strong> The remarkably flattened blue star at the river's end</li>
              <li><strong>Fomalhaut's debris disk:</strong> A dusty ring around a nearby star, a nursery for possible planets</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p>The Carina Nebula is the crown jewel: spanning several full-Moon widths of sky and glowing across a region hundreds of light-years wide, it dwarfs the Orion Nebula and contains some of the most massive, violent stars in the galaxy. For southern observers it is one of the unmissable sights of the night sky.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="observation" class="content-section">
          <h2>Observing the Heavenly Waters</h2>
          <p>How much of this family you can see depends strongly on your latitude, since most of it is a southern-sky spectacle.</p>
          <h3>From the Southern Hemisphere</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Find Canopus:</strong> This brilliant white star, second only to Sirius, is your anchor for Carina and the ship constellations.</li>
            <li><strong>Sweep the Milky Way:</strong> Puppis, Vela, and Carina lie in a glorious band of the galaxy; binoculars reveal cluster after cluster.</li>
            <li><strong>Seek the Carina Nebula:</strong> Under a dark sky it is visible to the naked eye as a bright patch of the Milky Way.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>From the Northern Hemisphere</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Delphinus:</strong> Look just off the Summer Triangle for the tiny leaping dolphin on summer evenings.</li>
            <li><strong>Fomalhaut:</strong> On autumn evenings, look low in the south for a lone bright star — that is Fomalhaut in Piscis Austrinus.</li>
            <li><strong>Eridanus:</strong> Trace the river's northern beginning near Rigel in Orion during winter, though its southern end stays hidden.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="facts" class="content-section">
          <h2>Interesting Facts About the Heavenly Waters</h2>
          <ul class="facts-list">
            <li><strong>The only retired constellation:</strong> Argo Navis is the sole classical constellation to have been broken up and removed from the modern list of 88.</li>
            <li><strong>A star that guides spacecraft:</strong> Canopus is so bright and well-placed that many space probes use a "Canopus star tracker" to orient themselves in deep space.</li>
            <li><strong>Eta Carinae's Great Eruption:</strong> In the 1840s this monster star flared to become the second-brightest star in the sky, ejecting the glowing Homunculus Nebula, yet it somehow survived — and may explode as a supernova at any time.</li>
            <li><strong>A backwards signature:</strong> The Delphinus star names Sualocin and Rotanev encode "Nicolaus Venator" reversed, a cheeky joke by an assistant at the Palermo Observatory.</li>
            <li><strong>The flattest star:</strong> Achernar, at the end of Eridanus, spins so rapidly that its equatorial diameter is roughly 50% larger than its polar diameter.</li>
            <li><strong>The Lonely Star:</strong> Fomalhaut appears to shine in solitude because the surrounding autumn sky is so sparse of bright stars — and it hosts one of the first debris disks ever imaged.</li>
            <li><strong>Shared star names across a ship:</strong> Because Argo was split up, the Greek-letter labels of its stars carry over between Carina, Puppis, and Vela, so no single fragment has a complete alphabet.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="resources" class="content-section">
          <h2>External Resources</h2>
          <ul class="resource-list">
            <li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA Webb Telescope</a> — Spectacular images of the Carina Nebula and Southern Ring Nebula</li>
            <li><a href="https://www.iau.org/public/themes/constellations/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IAU Constellations</a> — Official boundaries of the Heavenly Waters constellations</li>
            <li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argo_Navis" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Argo Navis on Wikipedia</a> — History of the great ship constellation and its division</li>
            <li><a href="https://www.messier.seds.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SEDS Messier Catalog</a> — Data on the open clusters of Puppis and other objects</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- FAQ Section -->
